  "name": "PNEUMOCYSTIS IFA",
  "code": "2038",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 25.0699%;\"><col style=\"width: 74.9301%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Result&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Remarks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive</td>\n<td>Indicates presence of Pneumocystis jeroveci cysts and trophozoites.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td>Indicates absence of Pneumocystis jeroveci cysts and trophozoites</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>1. Positive results are seen in Pneumocystis pneumonia, an opportunistic infection seen in&nbsp;immunocompromised patients particularly HIV positive individuals. Pneumocystis can also cause&nbsp;asymptomatic colonization of healthy as well as immunocompromised individuals.<br>2. 1-3 B D Glucan levels are generally elevated in Pneumocystis pneumonia.<br>3. Negative results are seen in absence of Pneumocystis jiroveci infection. However, it does not rule out the&nbsp;disease.<br>4. False positive results may be observed in presence of artefacts.<br>5. False-negative results may occur due to sampling errors or a low number of organisms in the specimen.<br>6. Test conducted in BAL/Sputum.</p>\n<p><strong>Uses</strong><br>Diagnosis of Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia</p>",
